export const N1={title:'The nursing process, clinical judgment and safe practice',hook:'A post-surgical patient says he feels “a little off.” A new nurse notes it and moves on; an experienced nurse checks his pulse, finds it racing and calls the surgeon before the patient goes into shock. The difference is clinical judgment: noticing cues, interpreting them, acting and reflecting. Basic care makes up a fifth of the Clinical Nursing test, and it begins here.',objectives:['Apply the five steps of the nursing process to a patient situation.','Distinguish subjective and objective data and write nursing diagnoses and goals.','Use clinical judgment, prioritization frameworks and SBAR communication.','Use reflective practice, including the REFLECT model, to evaluate clinical performance.'],sections:[
['The nursing process',[
'The **nursing process** is a systematic, patient-centered method of care, often remembered as **ADPIE**. **Assessment** gathers data through interview, physical examination and records. **Diagnosis** analyzes the data to identify health problems that nurses can treat, written as **nursing diagnoses** (for example, *Acute pain related to surgical incision as evidenced by patient rating pain 7 of 10*). **Planning** sets priorities and **SMART goals** (specific, measurable, achievable, realistic and time-bound) such as *Patient will report pain of 3 or less within 1 hour of medication*. **Implementation** carries out nursing interventions—independent (positioning, teaching), dependent (giving ordered medications) and collaborative (working with respiratory therapy). **Evaluation** determines whether goals were met and revises the plan. The process is cyclical: new data restart it.',
'**Subjective data** are what the patient reports (symptoms, feelings, history), such as “I feel dizzy.” **Objective data** are what the nurse observes or measures, such as a blood pressure of 88/50 mm Hg or a red, swollen incision. A **nursing diagnosis** differs from a **medical diagnosis**: pneumonia is a medical diagnosis; *Ineffective airway clearance* is a nursing diagnosis describing the patient’s response.',
sort('Subjective or objective data?',['Subjective','Objective'],[['“My chest feels tight.”',0],['Patient reports nausea since breakfast',0],['“I didn’t sleep last night.”',0],['Respiratory rate 28 breaths/min',1],['Incision edges red and warm',1],['Urine output 20 mL/h',1]])]],
['Clinical judgment and prioritization',[
'**Clinical judgment** is how nurses notice and act on what matters. The NCSBN Clinical Judgment Measurement Model describes steps used on licensing exams: **recognize cues**, **analyze cues**, **prioritize hypotheses**, **generate solutions**, **take action** and **evaluate outcomes**. Prioritization frameworks guide which problem comes first. The **ABCs**—**airway, breathing, circulation**—come before other needs. **Maslow’s hierarchy** places physiological needs first, then safety, love and belonging, esteem and self-actualization. Acute, unstable or unexpected changes come before chronic, stable or expected findings.',
'For example, among four patients—one with a new oxygen saturation of 85%, one requesting a pain medication, one needing discharge teaching and one with stable chronic edema—the nurse sees the patient with low oxygen first because it is an acute breathing problem.',
order('Prioritize these needs (most urgent first)',['Airway obstruction from secretions','Breathing: oxygen saturation 85%','Circulation: bleeding from an incision','Acute pain rated 8 of 10','Fear about tomorrow’s surgery','Discharge teaching for a stable patient'])]],
['Communication, safety and documentation',[
'Safe practice depends on clear communication. **SBAR** organizes urgent reports: **Situation** (what is happening now), **Background** (relevant history), **Assessment** (what the nurse thinks is wrong) and **Recommendation** (what the nurse needs). Handoffs between shifts use structured reports, and verbal orders are repeated back. **Two patient identifiers**, such as name and date of birth, are checked before every medication, procedure and specimen. Fall prevention includes assessing risk, keeping the bed low and locked, call light within reach, nonskid footwear and hourly rounding. **Restraints** are a last resort, require an order, must be the least restrictive option and are checked and released on schedule.',
'**Documentation** is legal evidence of care: record objectively, promptly and accurately; never chart care before it is done; correct errors according to policy without obliterating them. If it was not documented, it may be considered not done.',
match('Match the SBAR element',[['“Mr. Lee’s blood pressure dropped to 84/50.”','Situation'],['“He had abdominal surgery this morning.”','Background'],['“I think he may be bleeding internally.”','Assessment'],['“Please come evaluate him now; should I increase his IV fluids?”','Recommendation']])]],
['Reflective practice and the REFLECT model',[
'Nurses improve through **reflection**: thinking deliberately about experiences to learn from them. **Reflective journaling** turns clinical experiences into lessons. One widely taught framework is the **REFLECT** model: **R**ecall the events, **E**xamine your responses, acknowledge **F**eelings, **L**earn from the experience, **E**xplore options, **C**reate a plan of action and set a **T**imescale. The “F” stands for **Feelings**—the HOSA sample question—because recognizing emotions such as anxiety or frustration helps nurses understand how those feelings affected their actions. Reflection is honest but not self-punishing; its goal is a specific plan for next time.',
flip('The REFLECT model',[['R','Recall the events'],['E','Examine your responses'],['F','Acknowledge feelings'],['L','Learn from the experience'],['E','Explore options'],['C','Create a plan of action'],['T','Set a timescale']])]],
['Applying judgment to a changing patient',[
kase('A patient who “feels off”',{a:{text:'Two hours after abdominal surgery, Mr. Diaz says he feels anxious and “off.” What should the nurse do first?',choices:[['Assess vital signs, the incision and level of consciousness','b','Correct. Vague complaints can be early cues of deterioration.',true],['Reassure him that anxiety is normal after surgery','b','Dismissing cues can miss early shock.',false]]},b:{text:'His pulse is 124, blood pressure 92/58 (down from 128/76), skin cool and clammy, and the dressing is saturated. What next?',choices:[['Apply pressure to the dressing, keep him flat, and call the surgeon using SBAR','c','Correct. These are signs of hemorrhage and early shock.',true],['Document the findings and recheck in 4 hours','c','Signs of shock require immediate action.',false]]},c:{text:'The surgeon arrives, fluids are increased and Mr. Diaz returns to surgery. Afterward, the nurse writes a reflective journal entry using REFLECT, noting that early assessment of his “off” feeling mattered.',end:true}}),
'Notice how the nurse moved from cue to action. Restlessness, tachycardia, falling blood pressure, cool clammy skin and decreased urine output are classic signs of **hemorrhage and hypovolemic shock**, another HOSA sample topic. Acting early changes outcomes.']],
["Legal and ethical foundations of practice",["Each state’s **nurse practice act** defines the **scope of practice** for nursing roles and is enforced by the state **board of nursing**. A **certified nursing assistant (CNA)** provides basic care under supervision; a **licensed practical/vocational nurse (LPN/LVN)** completes about a year of training and works under the direction of an RN or provider; a **registered nurse (RN)**, prepared with an associate or bachelor’s degree and licensed after passing the **NCLEX-RN**, performs comprehensive assessment, care planning, teaching and evaluation; and an **advanced practice registered nurse (APRN)**—nurse practitioner, clinical nurse specialist, certified nurse midwife or certified registered nurse anesthetist—holds a graduate degree and may diagnose and prescribe according to state law.","RNs **delegate** tasks using the **five rights of delegation**: the right **task**, **circumstance**, **person**, **direction and communication** and **supervision and evaluation**. Assessment, teaching and evaluation of unstable patients are not delegated. **Negligence** is failure to use the care a reasonable nurse would use; **malpractice** is professional negligence, proven by four elements—**duty, breach of duty, causation and damages**. Protection comes from following policy, staying within scope, communicating changes and documenting accurately and promptly (“if it wasn’t documented, it wasn’t done”). **Informed consent** is obtained by the provider performing a procedure; the nurse may witness the signature and confirms the patient understands. **HIPAA** protects health information, and **incident (occurrence) reports** document unexpected events for quality improvement—they are not mentioned in the chart. Ethical principles guide decisions: **autonomy** (respecting choices), **beneficence** (doing good), **nonmaleficence** (avoiding harm), **justice** (fairness), **fidelity** (keeping promises) and **veracity** (truthfulness).",{"t":"check","q":"An RN asks a nursing assistant to take vital signs on a stable patient and report any pulse over 100. Which right of delegation is shown by the reporting instruction?","options":["Right direction and communication","Right circumstance","Right person","Right task"],"why":"Clear instructions with reporting limits are the right direction and communication."},{"t":"check","q":"Which element must be proven for malpractice in addition to duty, breach and causation?","options":["Damages (actual harm)","Intent to harm","A signed consent","A witness"],"why":"Malpractice requires duty, breach, causation and damages."}]]
],takeaways:['Nursing process: assessment, diagnosis, planning (SMART goals), implementation, evaluation.','Prioritize with ABCs and Maslow; clinical judgment moves from cues to action and evaluation; report with SBAR.','REFLECT: Recall, Examine, Feelings, Learn, Explore, Create, Timescale; postop shock shows tachycardia, hypotension, cool clammy skin and restlessness.'],terms:[['Nursing diagnosis','A clinical judgment about a patient’s response to a health problem.'],['SBAR','Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation communication tool.'],['Objective data','Information the nurse observes or measures.']],mcqs:[q('When using reflective journaling to self-evaluate clinical skills, what does the “F” in the REFLECT mode represent?','Feelings','Facts','Focus','Family','This is the HOSA sample question.'),q('Which is objective data?','Blood pressure 88/50 mm Hg','“I feel dizzy.”','Patient reports nausea','“My pain is a 7.”','Objective data are observed or measured.'),q('The first step of the nursing process is:','Assessment','Planning','Evaluation','Implementation','Data come first.'),q('According to the ABCs, which patient is seen first?','A patient with an oxygen saturation of 85%','A patient requesting a pain medication','A patient awaiting discharge teaching','A patient with stable chronic edema','Breathing problems take priority.'),q('Which signs suggest postoperative hemorrhage and shock?','Rapid pulse, falling blood pressure, cool clammy skin','Slow pulse, high blood pressure, warm dry skin','Increased urine output and flushed skin','Fever and itching','These reflect reduced circulating volume.'),q('In SBAR, the “R” is:','Recommendation','Result','Reason','Record','The nurse states what is needed.'),q('A SMART goal is:','Specific, measurable, achievable, realistic and time-bound','Short and medical','Set by the physician only','Always about pain','Goals must be measurable.'),q('Restraints should be:','The least restrictive option, ordered and checked on schedule','Used to prevent all falls','Applied without an order in any case','Left on indefinitely','They are a last resort.')],frq:{prompt:'A patient on the second postoperative day has a pulse of 118, blood pressure 94/60 and is restless. Apply the nursing process and clinical judgment, explain how you would communicate with the provider, and describe how you would use the REFLECT model afterward.',rubric:[['Assessment and cues',['assess','pulse','blood pressure','restless','incision','urine']],['Diagnosis, priority and actions',['shock','hemorrhage','priority','abc','circulation','fluids','flat']],['SBAR communication',['sbar','situation','background','assessment','recommendation']],['REFLECT model',['recall','feelings','learn','plan','timescale']]],model:'I would assess further: vital signs, level of consciousness, skin color and temperature, the incision and dressing, drains and urine output, recognizing tachycardia, hypotension and restlessness as cues of possible hemorrhage and hypovolemic shock. Using the ABCs, circulation is the priority, so I would keep the patient flat, apply pressure to any bleeding, ensure IV access and stay with him while calling the surgeon. I would use SBAR: the situation (pulse 118, BP 94/60, restless), background (second day after surgery), assessment (possible bleeding and shock) and recommendation (please evaluate now; consider fluids and labs). Afterward, I would use REFLECT: recall what happened, examine my responses, acknowledge feelings such as anxiety, learn what cues I noticed, explore other options, create a plan for future practice and set a timescale to practice it.'},sources:[RULES,NCSBN_CJ,AHRQ_SBAR]};

export const N2={title:'Infection control, asepsis and personal protective equipment',hook:'Health care–associated infections affect a significant number of hospitalized patients, and many are preventable with practices nurses perform hundreds of times a shift: cleaning hands, choosing the right protective equipment and keeping sterile items sterile. HOSA’s Skill VIII tests the PPE sequence that protects both patient and nurse.',objectives:['Explain the chain of infection and how nursing actions break it.','Distinguish medical asepsis from surgical asepsis and set up a sterile field.','Apply standard and transmission-based precautions.','Don and doff PPE in the correct sequence (HOSA Skill VIII).'],sections:[
['The chain of infection and HAIs',[
'Infection requires a **chain**: an infectious **agent**, a **reservoir**, a **portal of exit**, a **mode of transmission**, a **portal of entry** and a **susceptible host**. Hospitalized patients are especially susceptible because of wounds, invasive devices, chronic illness and immune suppression. **Health care–associated infections (HAIs)** include **catheter-associated urinary tract infections (CAUTI)**, **central line–associated bloodstream infections (CLABSI)**, **ventilator-associated pneumonia**, **surgical site infections** and *Clostridioides difficile* infection. Nurses break the chain with **hand hygiene**, cleaning equipment, using devices only when necessary and removing them promptly, and monitoring for signs of infection: fever, redness, swelling, warmth, drainage and elevated white blood cell count.',
'**Hand hygiene** is the single most important prevention measure. **Alcohol-based hand rub** is preferred for most situations when hands are not visibly soiled. **Soap and water** (at least 20 seconds of friction) are required when hands are visibly soiled and after caring for patients with *C. difficile* or norovirus, because alcohol does not reliably inactivate spores. The WHO’s **five moments** are before touching a patient, before a clean or aseptic procedure, after body fluid exposure risk, after touching a patient and after touching patient surroundings.',
sort('Hand rub or soap and water?',['Alcohol-based hand rub acceptable','Soap and water required'],[['Before entering a room when hands look clean',0],['After taking a blood pressure',0],['After caring for a patient with C. difficile',1],['When hands are visibly soiled with blood',1],['Before preparing a medication',0],['After a patient with norovirus vomits',1]])]],
['Medical and surgical asepsis',[
'**Medical asepsis** (clean technique) reduces the number and spread of microorganisms: hand hygiene, clean gloves, cleaning surfaces and keeping clean and dirty items separate. **Surgical asepsis** (sterile technique) eliminates all microorganisms from an area and is used for invasive procedures such as urinary catheterization, wound irrigation of open wounds and central line care. Principles of a **sterile field**: only sterile items touch sterile items; the outer **one-inch border** of a sterile field is considered contaminated; items below waist level or out of sight are contaminated; moisture wicks microorganisms through the field (strike-through), so it must stay dry; never turn your back on a sterile field or reach across it; and when in doubt, consider it contaminated.',
'To open a sterile package on a table, open the first flap **away** from you, then the side flaps, and the last flap **toward** you, touching only the outer surface. When pouring solutions, hold the bottle with the label in your palm and pour a small amount to waste first if the bottle was previously opened, then pour without splashing. Sterile gloves are put on by touching only the inside cuff of the first glove and sliding the gloved fingers under the cuff of the second.',
sort('Sterile or contaminated?',['Still sterile','Contaminated'],[['Item placed in the center of a dry sterile field',0],['Sterile glove touching another sterile item',0],['Item touching the one-inch border',1],['Sterile field that became wet',1],['Sterile item held below the waist',1],['Field left unattended out of sight',1]])]],
['Transmission-based precautions',[
'**Standard precautions** apply to all patients. Additional precautions match the route of spread. **Contact precautions**: gown and gloves on entry, dedicated equipment, for MRSA, VRE, *C. difficile* and scabies. **Droplet precautions**: a surgical mask within close range, private room when possible, for influenza, pertussis and meningococcal meningitis. **Airborne precautions**: a fit-tested **N95** respirator, an **airborne infection isolation room** with negative pressure and the door kept closed, for tuberculosis, measles and varicella. Patients transported outside the room wear a mask when on droplet or airborne precautions. Visitors receive teaching about required PPE.']],
['Donning and doffing PPE (HOSA Skill VIII)',[
'The CDC’s recommended sequence for **donning** is: perform hand hygiene; put on the **gown**, fastening the neck and waist ties; put on the **mask or respirator**, securing ties or elastic and fitting the flexible band to the nose (with a user seal check for a respirator); put on **goggles or a face shield**; and put on **gloves**, extending them over the gown cuffs. During care, keep hands away from the face, limit touched surfaces and change gloves when torn or heavily soiled.',
'For **doffing**, the most contaminated items come off first, and the outside of PPE is treated as contaminated. One CDC sequence is: remove **gloves** (grasp the outside of one glove at the palm, peel it off, hold it in the gloved hand, slide fingers of the ungloved hand under the remaining glove at the wrist and peel it off over the first), then **goggles or face shield** by the head band or earpieces, then the **gown** by unfastening ties and pulling it away from the neck and shoulders, touching only the inside and rolling it into a bundle, then the **mask or respirator** by the ties or elastics without touching the front, and finally **hand hygiene**. Hand hygiene is also performed immediately if hands become contaminated at any point. Remove PPE at the doorway or in an anteroom, and remove a respirator after leaving the room.',
order('Don PPE (CDC sequence)',['Perform hand hygiene','Gown','Mask or respirator','Goggles or face shield','Gloves over the gown cuffs']),
order('Doff PPE (CDC sequence)',['Gloves','Goggles or face shield','Gown','Mask or respirator','Hand hygiene'])]],
['Putting it into practice',[
kase('An isolation room',{a:{text:'A patient has C. difficile diarrhea. Which precautions apply?',choices:[['Contact precautions with gown and gloves, and soap-and-water hand hygiene','b','Correct. Spores spread by contact and resist alcohol.',true],['Airborne precautions with an N95','b','C. difficile is not airborne.',false]]},b:{text:'While wearing PPE you touch your goggles with a contaminated glove. What now?',choices:[['Consider the goggles contaminated; when leaving, remove PPE in order and perform hand hygiene','c','Correct. Contaminated items are handled as such during doffing.',true],['Wipe the glove on your gown and continue','c','This spreads contamination.',false]]},c:{text:'You doff gloves, goggles, gown and mask in order at the doorway, wash with soap and water and clean the stethoscope with an appropriate disinfectant.',end:true}}),
'Common PPE errors include touching the face with gloved hands, pulling a contaminated gown over the head, removing a mask by its front, and skipping hand hygiene between glove changes. Practicing the sequence until it is automatic prevents these errors under time pressure, as in HOSA’s five-minute skill.']],
["Cleaning, disinfection, sterilization and sharps safety",["Reusable items are processed according to how they will be used, a system called the **Spaulding classification**. **Critical** items that enter sterile tissue or the bloodstream—surgical instruments, implants—must be **sterilized**, usually by **steam under pressure (autoclave)** or by low-temperature methods such as ethylene oxide or hydrogen peroxide gas for heat-sensitive items. **Semicritical** items that touch mucous membranes, such as endoscopes, require **high-level disinfection**. **Noncritical** items that touch intact skin—blood pressure cuffs, stethoscopes, bed rails—need **low-level disinfection** with an approved disinfectant, observing the product’s **contact (wet) time**. Cleaning to remove organic material always comes first, because soil shields microorganisms. Sterilization indicators (tape or strips that change color) and biological indicators confirm the process worked.","**Sharps injuries** can transmit hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV. Prevention includes using **safety-engineered devices**, **never recapping used needles** (if recapping is unavoidable, use a one-handed scoop technique), disposing of sharps immediately in a **puncture-resistant sharps container** at the point of use and replacing containers when about three-quarters full. After a needlestick, the worker immediately washes the area with soap and water, reports the exposure and receives prompt evaluation for post-exposure prophylaxis. Healthcare workers are offered the **hepatitis B vaccine**. Spills of blood are cleaned with gloves, absorbent material and an EPA-registered disinfectant or freshly diluted bleach.",{"t":"check","q":"A stethoscope touches only intact skin. Which level of processing does it require?","options":["Low-level disinfection","Sterilization","High-level disinfection","No cleaning"],"why":"Noncritical items need low-level disinfection."}]]
],takeaways:['Hand hygiene is the most important infection prevention measure; use soap and water for visible soil, C. difficile and norovirus.','Surgical asepsis: only sterile touches sterile; the one-inch border, wet fields and items below the waist are contaminated.','Don: hand hygiene, gown, mask, goggles, gloves; doff: gloves, goggles, gown, mask, hand hygiene.'],terms:[['Surgical asepsis','Practices that keep an area free of all microorganisms.'],['Health care–associated infection','An infection acquired while receiving health care.'],['Strike-through','Contamination of a sterile field when moisture wicks through it.']],mcqs:[q('Which is the single most important measure to prevent health care–associated infections?','Hand hygiene','Wearing a gown','Antibiotic use','Private rooms','Clean hands break the chain.'),q('After caring for a patient with C. difficile, the nurse should:','Wash with soap and water','Use alcohol hand rub only','Use no hand hygiene if gloves were worn','Use a disinfectant wipe on hands','Alcohol does not reliably kill spores.'),q('What part of a sterile field is considered contaminated?','The outer one-inch border','The center','The entire field once opened','Only the corners','Edges are considered contaminated.'),q('Which PPE item is donned first in the CDC sequence?','Gown','Gloves','Mask','Goggles','Gloves are last.'),q('Which PPE item is removed first in the CDC sequence?','Gloves','Mask','Gown','Goggles','Gloves are the most contaminated.'),q('Measles requires which precautions?','Airborne','Contact','Droplet','Standard only','Measles spreads through the air.'),q('When opening a sterile package, the first flap is opened:','Away from you','Toward you','To the left','It does not matter','This avoids reaching across the field.'),q('Medical asepsis is also called:','Clean technique','Sterile technique','Isolation','Disinfection only','It reduces, rather than eliminates, microorganisms.')],frq:{prompt:'You must perform a sterile wound irrigation for a patient on contact precautions for MRSA. Explain the precautions required, how you would maintain surgical asepsis, and the order for donning and doffing PPE.',rubric:[['Contact precautions for MRSA',['contact','gown','gloves','mrsa','dedicated']],['Surgical asepsis principles',['sterile','one-inch','border','dry','below the waist','reach across']],['Donning order',['hand hygiene','gown','mask','goggles','gloves']],['Doffing order',['gloves','goggles','gown','mask','hand hygiene']]],model:'MRSA requires contact precautions: a gown and gloves on entering the room, dedicated equipment and careful hand hygiene, added to standard precautions; because irrigation can splash, I would also wear a mask and eye protection. For surgical asepsis, I would open the sterile tray away from myself first, keep the field dry, treat the one-inch border as contaminated, keep sterile items above my waist and in view, never reach across the field, and touch sterile items only with sterile gloves. I would don PPE in the CDC order: hand hygiene, gown, mask, goggles or face shield and gloves over the gown cuffs. To doff, I would remove gloves, then goggles, then the gown rolled inside out, then the mask by its ties, and finish with hand hygiene.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_PPE,CDC_HH]};

export const N3={title:'Medication administration and injections',hook:'A nurse may give dozens of medications in a shift, and each one passes through a series of checks designed to catch errors before they reach the patient. Injections add technique: the right needle, the right site, the right angle. HOSA’s Skills I-A, I-B and I-C test intramuscular, subcutaneous and intradermal injections step by step.',objectives:['Apply the rights of medication administration and medication safety checks.','Calculate doses accurately and prepare medication from a vial.','Compare intramuscular, subcutaneous and intradermal injections by angle, needle, site and volume.','Perform an injection following the HOSA rating sheet sequence.'],sections:[
['Rights and safety checks',[
'Nurses follow the **rights of medication administration**: the right **patient**, **drug**, **dose**, **route**, **time** and **documentation**, with additional rights often taught, such as right reason, right response and the patient’s right to refuse. The label is compared with the **medication administration record (MAR)** at least three times: when taking the medication from storage, when preparing it and at the bedside before giving it. The nurse checks the **expiration date**, allergies and relevant assessment data (such as heart rate before digoxin or blood glucose before insulin), and identifies the patient with **two identifiers** and the ID band. **High-alert medications**—such as insulin, heparin, opioids and concentrated potassium—carry greater risk of harm, and many facilities require an independent double check.',
'Errors are reported through the facility’s safety system so processes can be improved. Avoid error-prone abbreviations: write “units” rather than “U,” use a **leading zero** (0.5 mg) and **no trailing zero** (5 mg, not 5.0 mg).',
order('Medication safety checks in order',['Compare the order with the MAR','Check the label against the MAR when removing from storage','Check allergies, expiration date and assessment data','Calculate and prepare the dose, checking the label again','Identify the patient with two identifiers at the bedside','Check the label a third time and explain the medication','Administer and document; evaluate the response'])]],
['Calculating and preparing doses',[
'Dosage calculation uses **desired ÷ have × quantity** or **dimensional analysis**. If the order is 75 mg and the vial contains 100 mg/2 mL, the volume is 75 ÷ 100 × 2 = 1.5 mL. HOSA judges verify calculations. To withdraw from a **vial**, place it on a flat surface, remove the cover, **cleanse the rubber port with alcohol and let it dry**, draw air into the syringe **equal to the volume of medication to be withdrawn**, insert the needle through the center of the stopper and inject the air (to prevent a vacuum), invert the vial while holding the syringe and plunger firmly, withdraw the correct amount, then hold the syringe at **eye level** to verify the dose and remove air bubbles by tapping. Change the needle if indicated, cover it with the safety sheath and use sterile technique throughout. **Ampules** are glass containers that are snapped open away from the face, and medication is withdrawn with a **filter needle** to remove glass fragments.',
{t:'w',kind:'dosecalc',title:'Dose calculation practice',types:['liquid','weight'],prompt:'Work each problem on paper with units, then check. Round as directed.'}]],
['Comparing injection routes',[
{t:'table',caption:'Typical adult injection technique (follow facility policy and the order)',head:['Route','Angle','Common needle','Typical sites','Typical volume'],rows:[['Intradermal (ID)','10–15°, bevel up','26–27 gauge, ¼–½ inch','Inner forearm, upper back','About 0.01–0.1 mL'],['Subcutaneous (SubQ)','45–90° depending on tissue and needle length','25–30 gauge, ⅜–⅝ inch','Abdomen (about 2 inches from the umbilicus), back of upper arm, anterior thigh','Usually up to about 1 mL'],['Intramuscular (IM)','90°','22–25 gauge, 1–1½ inch (adult)','Ventrogluteal, deltoid, vastus lateralis','Deltoid up to about 1 mL; larger muscles up to about 3 mL']]},
'The **ventrogluteal** site is preferred for many adult IM injections because it is away from major nerves and vessels; it is located by placing the palm on the greater trochanter, the index finger on the anterior superior iliac spine and spreading the middle finger toward the iliac crest. The **deltoid** is used for small volumes such as vaccines; the **vastus lateralis** is preferred in infants. The **dorsogluteal** site is generally avoided because of the sciatic nerve. **Z-track** technique (pulling skin laterally before injecting and releasing after) seals irritating medications such as iron in the muscle.',
{t:'w',kind:'label',diagram:'injection',title:'Injection depths and angles',parts:[['epidermis','Epidermis','Outer skin layer.'],['dermis','Dermis','Intradermal injections deposit here, forming a small wheal.'],['subq','Subcutaneous tissue','Fatty layer where insulin and heparin are given.'],['muscle','Muscle','Intramuscular injections reach muscle at 90°.'],['id','Intradermal angle','10–15°, bevel up.'],['sc','Subcutaneous angle','45–90° depending on tissue.'],['im','Intramuscular angle','90°.']]}]],
['Giving the injection (HOSA Skills I-A to I-C)',[
'After preparing the medication, the competitor greets and identifies the patient by checking the ID band and asking the name, explains the skill, provides privacy, raises the bed, performs hand hygiene and puts on gloves, positions the patient for the chosen site and **inspects the site using anatomical landmarks**. The site is cleaned with alcohol in a **circular motion from the center outward about 2 inches** and allowed to dry. For an **IM injection**, the cap is pulled straight off, the skin held taut with the nondominant hand (or bunched if muscle mass is small), the syringe held like a dart at **90°** and the needle inserted quickly. HOSA’s sheet then calls for **aspirating** by pulling back on the plunger and, if blood appears, withdrawing and preparing a new injection (unless contraindicated, as with vaccines or heparin). Medication is injected slowly and steadily, the needle withdrawn at the same angle, gentle pressure applied with gauze **without massaging**, the safety sheath engaged and the syringe discarded in the sharps container.',
'For **subcutaneous** injections, the skin is often **pinched** and the needle inserted at 45–90°; heparin and insulin are not aspirated, and heparin sites are not massaged, to prevent bruising. For **intradermal** injections, such as a **tuberculin skin test**, the skin is stretched taut, the needle inserted **bevel up at 10–15°** just under the skin, and the medication injected slowly to form a small **wheal (bleb)**; the site is not massaged, may be circled, and a TB test is read in **48–72 hours** by measuring induration.',
sort('Which route?',['Intradermal','Subcutaneous','Intramuscular'],[['Tuberculin skin test forming a wheal',0],['Bevel up at 10–15°',0],['Insulin in the abdomen',1],['Heparin without aspirating or massaging',1],['Vaccine into the deltoid at 90°',2],['Ventrogluteal site with a 1½-inch needle',2]])]],
['After the injection',[
'Evaluate the patient’s response: relief of pain after an analgesic, changes in blood glucose after insulin, or signs of an allergic reaction such as hives, wheezing or swelling, which require immediate action. Document the medication, dose, route, site, time and response, and any patient teaching. If a patient refuses, explore the reason, provide information, respect a capable patient’s decision and notify the provider. In the event of a needlestick, wash the area, report it immediately and follow post-exposure procedures.',
kase('An unclear order',{a:{text:'An order reads “Insulin 10 U SubQ now.” The vial is U-100. What concern should the nurse raise?',choices:[['The abbreviation U can be misread; clarify and use “units,” and verify with a second nurse for this high-alert drug','b','Correct. U is on error-prone abbreviation lists and insulin is high-alert.',true],['No concern; give 1.0 mL','b','10 units of U-100 insulin is 0.1 mL; misreading U can cause a tenfold error.',false]]},b:{text:'After clarification, the order is 10 units. Which syringe is used?',choices:[['An insulin syringe marked in units','c','Correct. Insulin syringes match unit doses.',true],['A 3 mL syringe estimating 0.1 mL','c','Insulin syringes prevent dosing errors.',false]]},c:{text:'The nurse checks glucose, double-checks the dose with another nurse, injects into the abdomen without aspirating and documents the site and dose.',end:true}})]],
["Oral, topical, inhaled and intravenous routes",["Most medications are given by mouth. The nurse checks that the patient is alert and can swallow, positions them upright, gives tablets with water and stays until they are swallowed. **Enteric-coated** and **extended-release** tablets must not be crushed; when crushing is allowed for a patient with dysphagia, the nurse checks with the pharmacy. **Sublingual** medications such as nitroglycerin dissolve under the tongue, and **buccal** medications dissolve against the cheek, both entering the bloodstream rapidly. **Liquid** doses are measured at the bottom of the **meniscus** at eye level or with an oral syringe.","**Transdermal patches** deliver medication steadily; the old patch is removed, the site rotated, the new patch dated and initialed, and heat avoided over the patch. Eye drops go into the lower **conjunctival sac** while the patient looks up, followed by gentle pressure on the inner canthus. **Metered-dose inhalers** often require a **spacer** to improve delivery. **Intravenous** medications act fastest and therefore carry the greatest risk: the nurse checks compatibility, the IV site for patency, and the correct rate, and watches closely for reactions. IV push medications are given over the recommended time, never faster. High-alert medications such as insulin, heparin, opioids and potassium chloride require extra checks, and **concentrated potassium is never given by IV push**.",{"t":"check","q":"Which medication form should not be crushed?","options":["An extended-release tablet","A plain aspirin tablet","A scored tablet without coating","A chewable tablet"],"why":"Crushing extended-release forms can release a dangerous dose at once."}]]
],takeaways:['Check rights and labels three times; two identifiers; high-alert drugs get independent double checks; avoid “U” and trailing zeros.','Inject air equal to the volume into a vial; verify the dose at eye level; ampules need a filter needle.','ID 10–15° bevel up (wheal); SubQ 45–90° (no aspiration for insulin/heparin); IM 90° (ventrogluteal preferred, deltoid for small volumes); don’t massage.'],terms:[['MAR','Medication administration record.'],['Z-track','IM technique that seals medication in muscle.'],['Wheal','Small raised bleb formed by an intradermal injection.']],mcqs:[q('At what angle is an intramuscular injection given?','90°','10–15°','45°','30°','IM injections enter muscle perpendicular to the skin.'),q('An intradermal injection is given at:','10–15° with the bevel up','90°','45° with the bevel down','60°','It forms a wheal under the epidermis.'),q('Before withdrawing medication from a vial, the nurse injects:','Air equal to the volume to be withdrawn','Saline','Twice the volume of air','Nothing','This prevents a vacuum.'),q('Which site is preferred for many adult IM injections because it avoids major nerves and vessels?','Ventrogluteal','Dorsogluteal','Abdomen','Inner forearm','The dorsogluteal site risks the sciatic nerve.'),q('The order is 75 mg; the vial is 100 mg/2 mL. How many mL?','1.5 mL','0.75 mL','2.5 mL','1.33 mL','75 ÷ 100 × 2.'),q('When is a tuberculin skin test read?','48–72 hours','2 hours','1 week','Immediately','Induration is measured then.'),q('Which statement about heparin SubQ is correct?','Do not aspirate or massage the site','Always aspirate','Massage to speed absorption','Give at 10° bevel up','This prevents bruising.'),q('An ampule should be withdrawn with:','A filter needle','A larger syringe only','No needle','An insulin syringe','Filters remove glass fragments.')],frq:{prompt:'A patient is ordered 50 mg of a medication IM from a vial labeled 25 mg/mL. Calculate the dose and describe how to prepare and give the injection following HOSA Skill I-A, including safety checks, site selection and technique.',rubric:[['Calculation',['2 ml','50','25']],['Safety checks and identification',['mar','three','expiration','two identifiers','allergies']],['Vial preparation',['alcohol','air','invert','eye level','bubble']],['IM technique and site',['90','ventrogluteal','deltoid','taut','aspirat','pressure','sharps']]],model:'The volume is 50 mg ÷ 25 mg/mL = 2 mL. I would compare the order with the MAR, check the drug label three times, its expiration date and the patient’s allergies, calculate the dose, then clean the vial port with alcohol, draw up 2 mL of air and inject it into the vial, invert it and withdraw 2 mL, checking at eye level for the correct dose and no bubbles. At the bedside, I would identify the patient with the ID band and name, explain the procedure, provide privacy, perform hand hygiene and put on gloves. For 2 mL, I would choose the ventrogluteal site using landmarks, clean it in a circle from the center outward, hold the skin taut, insert the needle quickly at 90°, aspirate per the rating sheet, inject slowly, withdraw at the same angle, apply gentle pressure without massaging, engage the safety device, discard the syringe in the sharps container and document the site and response.'},sources:[RULES,ISMP,CDC_INJ]};

export const N4={title:'Fluids, electrolytes, nutrition and nasogastric tubes',hook:'An older adult with vomiting becomes confused; a patient with heart failure gains three pounds overnight; a stroke patient cannot swallow safely. Each involves fluid, electrolyte or nutrition problems that nurses detect early and manage. Inserting a nasogastric tube, HOSA Skill II, is one of the tools.',objectives:['Assess fluid volume deficit and excess and monitor intake and output.','Recognize signs of sodium, potassium and calcium imbalances.','Describe therapeutic diets and enteral nutrition.','Insert a nasogastric tube following HOSA Skill II, including measurement and placement verification.'],sections:[
['Fluid balance',[
'Water makes up roughly half to 60% of adult body weight, divided between intracellular and extracellular compartments. Nurses monitor **intake and output (I&O)**—oral and IV fluids, tube feedings, urine, emesis, drainage—and **daily weights**, the most sensitive indicator of fluid change: a gain of about 1 kg (2.2 lb) reflects about 1 liter of fluid. Adult urine output should be at least about **30 mL per hour** (or 0.5 mL/kg/h). **Fluid volume deficit** (dehydration, hypovolemia) shows thirst, dry mucous membranes, poor skin turgor, rapid weak pulse, low blood pressure, dark concentrated urine and decreased output. **Fluid volume excess** (overload) shows weight gain, edema, high blood pressure, bounding pulse, crackles in the lungs, shortness of breath and distended neck veins; it is common in heart failure and kidney failure.',
sort('Deficit or excess?',['Fluid volume deficit','Fluid volume excess'],[['Dry mucous membranes and thirst',0],['Dark, concentrated urine',0],['Rapid, weak pulse',0],['Crackles in the lungs',1],['Pitting edema of the ankles',1],['Weight gain of 2 kg in a day',1]])]],
['Electrolyte imbalances',[
'Electrolytes have narrow normal ranges. **Sodium** (about 135–145 mEq/L) governs water balance and nerve function: **hyponatremia** causes confusion, headache and seizures; **hypernatremia** causes thirst, restlessness and dry mucous membranes. **Potassium** (about 3.5–5.0 mEq/L) affects heart and muscle: **hypokalemia** causes muscle weakness, leg cramps and flattened T waves or U waves on the ECG, often from diuretics or vomiting; **hyperkalemia** causes **peaked T waves** and dangerous arrhythmias, often in kidney failure. **Calcium** (about 8.5–10.5 mg/dL) affects nerves and muscles: **hypocalcemia** causes tingling, muscle spasms and positive **Chvostek** (facial twitch) and **Trousseau** (carpal spasm with a BP cuff) signs; **hypercalcemia** causes weakness, constipation and kidney stones. Potassium is never given IV push.',
match('Match the imbalance to its sign',[['Peaked T waves','Hyperkalemia'],['Muscle weakness and leg cramps after diuretics','Hypokalemia'],['Confusion and seizures','Hyponatremia'],['Positive Chvostek and Trousseau signs','Hypocalcemia'],['Constipation and kidney stones','Hypercalcemia'],['Thirst with dry mucous membranes and high sodium','Hypernatremia']])]],
['Nutrition and therapeutic diets',[
'Nutrition supports healing. Nurses screen for malnutrition, monitor weight and intake and collaborate with dietitians. **Therapeutic diets** include **clear liquid** (broth, gelatin, apple juice; often after surgery), **full liquid** (adds milk, pudding), **mechanical soft** or **pureed** (for chewing or swallowing problems), **low-sodium** (heart failure, hypertension), **consistent carbohydrate** (diabetes), **renal** (limited potassium, phosphorus and protein), **high-fiber** (constipation) and **low-fiber** (some bowel conditions). Patients with **dysphagia** after stroke need a swallow screen before oral intake, upright positioning and sometimes thickened liquids to prevent **aspiration**.',
'When patients cannot eat enough but the gut works, **enteral nutrition** delivers formula through a tube: nasogastric, nasoduodenal or a surgically placed gastrostomy (G-tube or PEG). If the gut cannot be used, **parenteral nutrition** delivers nutrients intravenously, usually through a central line. For tube feedings, keep the head of the bed **at least 30–45°** during feeding and for a period afterward, check placement and tolerance, flush tubes with water as ordered and monitor for diarrhea, distension and aspiration.']],
['Inserting a nasogastric tube (HOSA Skill II)',[
'A **nasogastric (NG) tube** passes through the nose into the stomach for **feeding** or for **decompression and suction** (removing gas and fluid). In Skill II, the competitor performs hand hygiene, checks the order for type, size and purpose, assembles and **tests the feeding or suction equipment**, greets and identifies the patient with **two identifiers**, explains the purpose and provides privacy, performs hand hygiene and puts on **nonsterile gloves**. The patient is placed in **high (full) Fowler’s position** if not contraindicated, with a towel across the chest. The tube is **measured** from the **tip of the nose to the earlobe**, then from the **earlobe to the xiphoid process** (the **NEX** measurement), and the point marked with tape. The face is wiped with a damp washcloth, the nose cleaned if the judge states it is needed, and the **nares checked for obstruction** by closing one nostril at a time; an obstructed nostril is avoided.',
'The competitor **lubricates 4–8 inches** of the tube with **water-soluble lubricant**, tilts the nose tip upward and inserts the tube gently along the nasal floor to the back of the throat. At the **nasopharynx**, the patient **lowers the head (chin toward chest)**, holds water with a straw, breathes through the mouth and **swallows as the tube is advanced** until the mark is reached. If **coughing, persistent gagging, cyanosis or dyspnea** occur, the tube is **removed immediately** and insertion attempted in the other nostril—judges ask this question. The tube is taped temporarily, the throat checked with a tongue blade and flashlight to ensure it is not coiled, and placement is confirmed—the gold standard is an **X-ray**, which the judge states. The tube is secured to the nose with prepared tape and to the gown, then connected as ordered.',
order('Insert an NG tube (HOSA Skill II)',['Check the order and test the feeding or suction equipment','Identify the patient, explain and provide privacy','Hand hygiene, gloves and high Fowler’s position','Measure nose to earlobe to xiphoid and mark','Check nares for obstruction','Lubricate 4–8 inches and insert to the nasopharynx','Have the patient lower the head and swallow as you advance to the mark','Check the throat, confirm placement by X-ray and secure the tube'])]],
['Applying the concepts',[
kase('A confused older adult',{a:{text:'An 82-year-old with three days of vomiting is newly confused. Blood pressure 96/58, pulse 112, mucous membranes dry, urine output 15 mL/h. What problem is most likely?',choices:[['Fluid volume deficit, possibly with an electrolyte imbalance','b','Correct. These are signs of dehydration.',true],['Fluid volume excess','b','Edema and crackles would be expected with excess.',false]]},b:{text:'Labs show sodium 128 mEq/L and potassium 3.0 mEq/L. Which findings fit?',choices:[['Hyponatremia contributing to confusion and hypokalemia causing weakness','c','Correct. Low sodium affects the brain; low potassium affects muscles and heart.',true],['Hyperkalemia with peaked T waves','c','Potassium is low, not high.',false]]},c:{text:'The provider orders IV fluids with potassium replacement by infusion. The nurse monitors I&O, daily weight, mental status and the cardiac rhythm, and confusion improves.',end:true}})]],
["IV therapy and enteral feeding safety",["IV fluids are classified by their concentration compared with blood. **Isotonic** solutions such as **0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline)** and **lactated Ringer’s** stay in the vascular space and replace volume; they are used for dehydration, blood loss and with blood transfusions (normal saline only). **Hypotonic** solutions such as **0.45% sodium chloride** move water into cells and treat cellular dehydration, but can worsen increased intracranial pressure. **Hypertonic** solutions such as **3% sodium chloride** pull water out of cells and require close monitoring. Dextrose 5% in water is isotonic in the bag but becomes hypotonic once the glucose is used.","IV sites are assessed regularly. **Infiltration** is leakage of fluid into the tissue: the site is **cool, pale, swollen** and the flow slows. **Phlebitis** is vein inflammation: the site is **red, warm and tender**, sometimes with a red streak. In either case, the nurse stops the infusion, removes the catheter, elevates the extremity and restarts in another site. **Fluid overload** shows as crackles, dyspnea, rising blood pressure and distended neck veins.","For **enteral (tube) feedings**, the head of the bed is kept elevated **at least 30–45 degrees** during and after feeding to reduce aspiration, tube placement and residual volume are checked per facility policy, the tube is flushed with water before and after feedings and medications, and medications are given one at a time. Signs of feeding intolerance include nausea, abdominal distension, diarrhea and high residuals.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Infiltration or phlebitis?","buckets":["Infiltration","Phlebitis"],"items":[["Cool, pale skin at the site",0],["Swelling with slowed flow",0],["Red streak along the vein",1],["Warm, red and tender site",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Daily weight is the best fluid indicator (1 kg ≈ 1 L); urine output at least about 30 mL/h.','Hyperkalemia: peaked T waves; hypokalemia: weakness, U waves; hyponatremia: confusion; hypocalcemia: Chvostek and Trousseau.','NG tube: high Fowler’s, measure NEX, lubricate 4–8 inches, swallow to advance, remove for coughing or cyanosis, confirm by X-ray.'],terms:[['Intake and output','Measurement of all fluids taken in and lost.'],['NEX measurement','Nose to earlobe to xiphoid process; NG tube length.'],['Enteral nutrition','Feeding through the gastrointestinal tract, often by tube.']],mcqs:[q('Which is the most sensitive indicator of fluid balance?','Daily weight','Blood pressure','Skin color','Pulse','1 kg ≈ 1 L of fluid.'),q('Peaked T waves on an ECG suggest:','Hyperkalemia','Hypokalemia','Hyponatremia','Hypercalcemia','High potassium alters repolarization.'),q('A positive Trousseau sign suggests:','Hypocalcemia','Hypercalcemia','Hyperkalemia','Hypernatremia','Low calcium causes carpal spasm.'),q('How is NG tube length measured?','Nose to earlobe to xiphoid process','Mouth to chin','Nose to navel','Ear to shoulder','This is the NEX method.'),q('How much of the NG tube is lubricated in HOSA Skill II?','4–8 inches','1 inch','12–18 inches','The entire tube','Water-soluble lubricant is used.'),q('If a patient becomes cyanotic during NG insertion, the nurse should:','Remove the tube immediately','Advance faster','Have the patient lie flat','Ignore it','The tube may be in the airway.'),q('The gold standard for confirming NG tube placement is:','X-ray','Listening to air instilled','Asking the patient','Measuring the tube','Radiographic confirmation is most reliable.'),q('Signs of fluid volume excess include:','Crackles, edema and weight gain','Dry mucous membranes and thirst','Dark concentrated urine','Weak, rapid pulse','Excess fluid accumulates.')],frq:{prompt:'A stroke patient with dysphagia needs a nasogastric tube for feeding. Explain why, describe the insertion procedure from HOSA Skill II including safety checks, and describe how you would monitor the patient’s fluid, electrolyte and nutrition status afterward.',rubric:[['Rationale: dysphagia and aspiration',['dysphagia','aspiration','enteral','swallow']],['Insertion steps',['fowler','nose','earlobe','xiphoid','lubricat','swallow']],['Safety and placement',['two identifiers','obstruct','cyanosis','remove','x-ray']],['Monitoring',['intake and output','weight','electrolyte','head of bed','30']]],model:'A stroke patient with dysphagia cannot swallow safely, so oral intake risks aspiration; enteral feeding through an NG tube provides nutrition while the gut functions. I would check the order, test the feeding equipment, identify the patient with two identifiers, explain the procedure and provide privacy, perform hand hygiene and put on gloves, then place the patient in high Fowler’s position. I would measure the tube from the nose to the earlobe to the xiphoid process and mark it, check the nares for obstruction, lubricate 4–8 inches with water-soluble lubricant, insert to the nasopharynx, have the patient lower the head and swallow as I advance to the mark, and remove the tube immediately for coughing, persistent gagging, cyanosis or dyspnea. I would check the throat, have placement confirmed by X-ray and secure the tube. Afterward I would keep the head of the bed at least 30–45°, monitor intake and output and daily weights, watch electrolytes and signs of dehydration or overload, and check feeding tolerance.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_ELECT,ASPEN]};

export const N5={title:'Perioperative care, wound care and postoperative exercises',hook:'Surgery succeeds in the operating room, but recovery depends on nursing care afterward: preventing pneumonia with deep breathing, preventing clots with early movement, keeping wounds clean and catching bleeding early. HOSA tests two key postoperative skills—assisting with exercises and sterile wound irrigation.',objectives:['Describe preoperative preparation and informed consent responsibilities.','Recognize and prevent postoperative complications such as hemorrhage, atelectasis and venous thromboembolism.','Teach splinting, coughing, deep breathing, huffing and incentive spirometry (HOSA Skill VI).','Assess wounds, stage pressure injuries and perform a sterile wound irrigation (HOSA Skill IV).'],sections:[
['Before surgery',[
'**Preoperative** care prepares the patient physically and emotionally. The surgeon obtains **informed consent**; the nurse confirms the form is signed, may witness the signature and reports if the patient does not understand. Preparation includes **nothing by mouth (NPO)** instructions as ordered, removal of jewelry and dentures, a preoperative checklist, baseline vital signs and labs, and **teaching** of postoperative exercises so the patient can practice them before pain and sedation make learning harder. The **surgical safety checklist** includes a **time-out** before incision to confirm the correct patient, procedure and site.']],
['Postoperative complications',[
'After surgery, nurses monitor vital signs frequently, level of consciousness, pain, the dressing and drains, intake and output, and return of bowel sounds. Key complications: **Hemorrhage and hypovolemic shock**—restlessness, tachycardia, falling blood pressure, cool clammy pale skin, decreased urine output and a saturated dressing. **Atelectasis and pneumonia**—shallow breathing from pain and anesthesia causes alveoli to collapse; signs include fever, decreased breath sounds and low oxygen saturation. **Venous thromboembolism (VTE)**—deep vein thrombosis causing calf pain, swelling and warmth, which can lead to **pulmonary embolism** with sudden shortness of breath and chest pain; prevention includes early ambulation, leg exercises, compression devices and anticoagulants. **Surgical site infection**, **urinary retention**, **paralytic ileus** and **wound dehiscence** (separation of the incision) or **evisceration** (organs protruding, an emergency covered with sterile saline-moistened dressings) also occur.',
{t:'w',kind:'vitals',title:'Watch the body compensate for blood loss',prompt:'Increase blood loss and watch heart rate rise before blood pressure falls. Which vital sign changes first?'},
sort('Match the finding to the complication',['Hemorrhage or shock','Atelectasis or pneumonia','Deep vein thrombosis','Wound dehiscence'],[['Tachycardia, hypotension and cool clammy skin',0],['Saturated dressing and restlessness',0],['Low-grade fever and diminished breath sounds at the bases',1],['Oxygen saturation drops with shallow breathing',1],['Calf swelling, warmth and pain',2],['Incision edges separate after coughing',3]])]],
['Postoperative exercises (HOSA Skill VI)',[
'Skill VI begins with hand hygiene, checking the order, identifying the patient with two identifiers, explaining the purpose and providing privacy, with gloves if there is drainage. **Splinting** an abdominal or chest incision distributes pressure and reduces pain: the nurse grasps a pillow, folded bath blanket or large towel at the edges, stretches it across the incision and **applies firm pressure** as the patient coughs—pushing down for a patient in bed or pulling the splint toward the nurse from behind for a seated patient. **Turning, coughing and deep breathing (TCDB)**: the patient takes a deep breath, **holds it 2–5 seconds**, then performs a **double cough with the mouth open**, repeated several times each hour. **Huffing** clears secretions with less strain: a deep abdominal breath forced out in several short, quick breaths, then a deeper breath with short panting exhalations, then an even deeper breath exhaled in a strong huff, repeated as ordered.',
'**Incentive spirometry** encourages deep breaths that reopen alveoli. The patient sits as upright as comfortable; the nurse explains the device and sets the **goal** in seconds or volume from the order; the patient coughs first to clear mucus, then seals the lips on the mouthpiece and takes **slow, deep breaths**, **holding each 2–5 seconds**, repeating until the goal is reached or best effort is given **at least 8–10 times**. The nurse documents the volumes achieved and teaches the patient to continue regularly while awake.',
order('Incentive spirometry steps (HOSA Skill VI)',['Position the patient as upright as possible','Explain how the spirometer works','Set the goal from the order','Have the patient cough to clear mucus','Inhale slowly and deeply, holding 2–5 seconds','Repeat 8–10 times or until the goal is reached','Document and report results'])]],
['Wounds and pressure injuries',[
'Wounds heal by **primary intention** (clean edges closed with sutures or staples), **secondary intention** (open wounds that fill with granulation tissue) or **tertiary intention** (delayed closure). Nurses assess size, depth, wound bed color (red granulation is healthy; yellow slough or black eschar indicate dead tissue), **drainage**—**serous** (clear), **sanguineous** (bloody), **serosanguineous** (pink) or **purulent** (thick, cloudy, infected)—odor, surrounding skin and pain. **Pressure injuries** develop over bony prominences from pressure and shear and are staged: **Stage 1**, intact skin with non-blanchable redness; **Stage 2**, partial-thickness skin loss or a blister; **Stage 3**, full-thickness skin loss with visible fat; **Stage 4**, full-thickness tissue loss exposing muscle, tendon or bone; **unstageable**, base obscured by slough or eschar; and **deep tissue injury**, purple or maroon intact skin. Prevention includes repositioning at least every 2 hours, pressure-redistributing surfaces, moisture control, nutrition and the **Braden Scale** risk assessment.',
match('Match the pressure injury stage',[['Intact skin with non-blanchable redness','Stage 1'],['Partial-thickness loss or intact blister','Stage 2'],['Full-thickness loss with visible fat','Stage 3'],['Exposed muscle, tendon or bone','Stage 4'],['Base covered by slough or eschar','Unstageable'],['Purple or maroon intact skin','Deep tissue injury']])]],
['Sterile wound irrigation (HOSA Skill IV)',[
'Irrigation cleans debris and drainage from an open wound. In Skill IV, after hand hygiene, checking the order, identifying the patient with two identifiers, explaining and providing privacy, the competitor puts on **gloves and an eye shield or face guard**, positions the patient so the solution **runs from the upper end of the wound downward**, places a waterproof pad and clean basin underneath and drapes to expose only the wound. The old dressing is removed and discarded with the gloves, and hand hygiene is repeated. The **irrigation tray** is opened with sterile technique; the solution bottle is opened with the cap inside-up; a small amount is poured off if the bottle was previously opened; solution is poured into the irrigation container; and the supply bottle is **dated and initialed with the patient’s name and ID**. After opening the sterile dressing and donning **sterile gloves**, the competitor prepares the tray and **assesses the drainage, wound size and condition and surrounding tissue**.',
'Solution is drawn into the irrigation syringe, which is held **just above the top edge of the wound**, and fluid is forced in **slowly and continuously** with enough force to flush debris **without squirting or splashing**, irrigating all parts of the wound without forcing solution into pockets, **until the solution draining from the bottom is clear**. The wound edges are patted dry with sterile 4×4 gauze **from the cleanest to the most contaminated area**, a sterile dressing is applied as ordered, PPE and supplies are removed and disposed of properly and hand hygiene is performed, with documentation of the wound’s appearance and the patient’s tolerance.',
kase('During a wound irrigation',{a:{text:'While irrigating, you notice the drainage at the bottom of the wound is still cloudy. What should you do?',choices:[['Continue irrigating gently until the returning solution is clear','b','Correct. The endpoint is clear drainage.',true],['Increase force and squirt into the deep pocket','b','Forcing solution into pockets can spread bacteria and damage tissue.',false]]},b:{text:'You see thick, yellow, foul-smelling drainage and redness spreading around the wound. What next?',choices:[['Complete the dressing, document the findings and report signs of infection to the provider','c','Correct. Purulent drainage and spreading redness suggest infection.',true],['Ignore it because irrigation cleaned the wound','c','Signs of infection must be reported.',false]]},c:{text:'The provider orders a wound culture and antibiotics. The nurse documents wound measurements, drainage and the patient’s tolerance of the procedure.',end:true}})]],
["Pain assessment and management",["Pain is what the patient says it is. Nurses assess **onset, location, duration, character, aggravating and relieving factors** and **intensity** with a numeric scale (0–10), a faces scale for children, or behavioral scales for patients who cannot report. Pain is reassessed after interventions, commonly 30–60 minutes after oral medication and 15–30 minutes after IV medication. **Multimodal** management combines medications—acetaminophen, NSAIDs and, when needed, opioids—with nonpharmacologic methods such as positioning, splinting the incision with a pillow during coughing, cold or heat as ordered, relaxation, distraction and music. **Patient-controlled analgesia (PCA)** lets the patient press a button for a limited dose; only the patient presses it.","Opioids can cause **respiratory depression**, sedation, constipation, nausea and itching. The nurse monitors respiratory rate, oxygen saturation and **sedation level**—increasing sedation comes before respiratory depression—and keeps **naloxone** available as the reversal agent. A bowel regimen prevents constipation. Controlled substances are counted and wasted with a witness according to policy.",{"t":"check","q":"A postoperative patient on opioids is difficult to arouse with respirations of 8 per minute. Which medication reverses opioids?","options":["Naloxone","Flumazenil","Vitamin K","Protamine"],"why":"Naloxone reverses opioid effects; support breathing and call for help."}]]
],takeaways:['Postop: watch for hemorrhage/shock, atelectasis, VTE, infection and dehiscence; early ambulation prevents complications.','Skill VI: splint the incision, deep breath hold 2–5 s with double cough, huffing, incentive spirometry 8–10 times holding 2–5 s.','Skill IV: position for downward flow, sterile tray, irrigate slowly from the top until clear, pat dry clean to dirty; stage pressure injuries 1–4, unstageable, DTI.'],terms:[['Atelectasis','Collapse of alveoli, common after surgery.'],['Dehiscence','Separation of a surgical wound’s edges.'],['Incentive spirometer','Device that encourages slow, deep breaths.']],mcqs:[q('A patient has just undergone a right nephrectomy. Which signs indicate postoperative hemorrhage and shock?','Rapid pulse, falling blood pressure, cool clammy skin and restlessness','Slow pulse and warm dry skin','High blood pressure and increased urine output','Fever and flushed skin only','These reflect reduced circulating volume.'),q('Splinting an incision during coughing:','Distributes pressure and reduces pain','Increases infection risk','Is used only for leg incisions','Replaces deep breathing','It supports the incision.'),q('During incentive spirometry, each deep breath is held for:','2–5 seconds','30 seconds','1 minute','No hold','HOSA Skill VI specifies 2–5 seconds.'),q('A Stage 2 pressure injury is:','Partial-thickness skin loss or a blister','Intact skin with non-blanchable redness','Exposed bone','Covered by eschar','Stage 1 has intact skin.'),q('During wound irrigation, the solution should flow:','From the upper end of the wound downward','From the bottom up','Into pockets with force','In any direction','Clean to dirty by gravity.'),q('When is wound irrigation complete?','When the draining solution is clear','After one syringe','After 30 seconds','When the patient asks','Clear return is the endpoint.'),q('Thick, cloudy, foul-smelling drainage is described as:','Purulent','Serous','Sanguineous','Serosanguineous','It suggests infection.'),q('Which measure helps prevent deep vein thrombosis after surgery?','Early ambulation and leg exercises','Bed rest with knees bent','Keeping the patient NPO','Withholding fluids','Movement promotes venous return.')],frq:{prompt:'A patient on the first day after abdominal surgery has shallow breathing and a draining incision. Describe how you would teach postoperative exercises following HOSA Skill VI and perform a sterile wound irrigation following Skill IV, and list the complications you are preventing.',rubric:[['Postoperative exercises',['splint','deep breath','2-5','2–5','double cough','huff','incentive']],['Wound irrigation technique',['sterile','top','downward','slowly','clear','4x4','4×4']],['Patient safety steps',['two identifiers','privacy','hand hygiene','eye shield','date']],['Complications prevented',['atelectasis','pneumonia','infection','dvt','dehiscence']]],model:'After identifying the patient with two identifiers, explaining and providing privacy, I would teach splinting by holding a pillow firmly across the incision while he coughs, then have him take a deep breath, hold it 2–5 seconds and double cough with his mouth open several times an hour, use huffing breaths and use the incentive spirometer sitting upright, inhaling slowly and holding 2–5 seconds at least 8–10 times toward the ordered goal. For the irrigation, I would wear gloves and an eye shield, position him so solution flows from the top of the wound downward, remove the old dressing, perform hand hygiene, open the sterile tray, date and initial the solution bottle, put on sterile gloves, assess the wound and irrigate slowly and continuously from just above the top edge without splashing until the drainage is clear, then pat dry from clean to contaminated with sterile 4×4s and apply a sterile dressing. These actions prevent atelectasis and pneumonia, surgical site infection and wound complications, and early movement also helps prevent deep vein thrombosis.'},sources:[RULES,AHRQ_PU,CDC_SSI]};
